tests/ui/types/ok_cholesky_rewrite.sky:8:5: note: `A⁻¹ @ b` is rewritten to a Cholesky solve
  |
8 | x = A⁻¹ @ b
  |     ^^^^^^^
  |     - positive definite
  = note: rewrite `A⁻¹ @ b` to a Cholesky solve: A is Matrix[3,3] and PositiveDefinite, b is Vector[3]. Factor A = L·Lᵀ (L lower-triangular), solve L·y = b, then Lᵀ·x = y. x = A⁻¹·b is Vector[3], and A⁻¹ is never formed.

tests/ui/types/ok_cholesky_rewrite.sky:9:5: note: `inv(A) @ b` is rewritten to a Cholesky solve
  |
9 | y = inv(A) @ b
  |     ^^^^^^^^^^
  |         - positive definite
  = note: rewrite `A⁻¹ @ b` to a Cholesky solve: A is Matrix[3,3] and PositiveDefinite, b is Vector[3]. Factor A = L·Lᵀ (L lower-triangular), solve L·y = b, then Lᵀ·x = y. x = A⁻¹·b is Vector[3], and A⁻¹ is never formed.

tests/ui/types/ok_cholesky_rewrite.sky:9:5: note: [GVN-001] `inv(A) @ b` at line 9 reuses the value computed at line 8
  |
9 | y = inv(A) @ b
  |     ^^^^^^^^^^
8 | x = A⁻¹ @ b
  |     ------- computed here first
  = note: applied by the `gvn` pass
  = note: because the earlier `solve` has the same operands and runs first on every path to this one, so both compute the same value
  = note: because `solve` can fail, but with the same operands the earlier one has failed already if this one would, and Sky reports the first failure, so this one can never be the failure that is reported

tests/ui/types/ok_cholesky_rewrite.sky:10:5: note: `K⁻¹ @ f` is rewritten to a Cholesky solve
   |
10 | u = K⁻¹ @ f
   |     ^^^^^^^
   |     - positive definite
   = note: rewrite `K⁻¹ @ f` to a Cholesky solve: K is Matrix[3,3] and PositiveDefinite, f is Vector[3]. Factor K = L·Lᵀ (L lower-triangular), solve L·y = f, then Lᵀ·x = y. x = K⁻¹·f is Vector[3], and K⁻¹ is never formed.

tests/ui/types/ok_cholesky_rewrite.sky:11:5: note: `inv(K⁻¹) @ (f + f)` is rewritten to a Cholesky solve
   |
11 | w = inv(K⁻¹) @ (f + f)
   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
   |         --- positive definite
   = note: rewrite `(K⁻¹)⁻¹ @ (f + f)` to a Cholesky solve: K⁻¹ is Matrix[3,3] and PositiveDefinite, (f + f) is Vector[3]. Factor (K⁻¹) = L·Lᵀ (L lower-triangular), solve L·y = (f + f), then Lᵀ·x = y. x = (K⁻¹)⁻¹·(f + f) is Vector[3], and (K⁻¹)⁻¹ is never formed.

tests/ui/types/ok_cholesky_rewrite.sky:11:17: note: [ALG-015] `f + f` is rewritten to `2 * f`
   |
11 | w = inv(K⁻¹) @ (f + f)
   |                 ^^^^^
   = note: applied by the `egraph` pass
   = note: because for an array, x + x and 2 * x are both the correctly rounded 2x in every entry (-0 included) or overflow in the same entries, and the product reads the array once

tests/ui/types/ok_cholesky_rewrite.sky:11:17: note: [EG-COST-001] `f + f` is rewritten to `2 * f`
   |
11 | w = inv(K⁻¹) @ (f + f)
   |                 ^^^^^
   = note: applied by the `egraph` pass
   = note: because chose `2 * f` over `f + f`: 3 vs 3 flops, 48 vs 72 bytes (weighted cost 73 vs 76)

tests/ui/types/ok_cholesky_rewrite.sky:11:17: note: [PAR-003] this elementwise `mul` over 3 elements is not parallelized: it may fail and is small
   |
11 | w = inv(K⁻¹) @ (f + f)
   |                 ^^^^^
   = note: because 3 elements is under the threshold of 262144: splitting it across threads costs more than it saves

tests/ui/types/ok_cholesky_rewrite.sky:11:17: note: [MEM-001] `f + f` reuses `f`'s buffer: no allocation for this result
   |
11 | w = inv(K⁻¹) @ (f + f)
   |                 ^^^^^
   = note: `f` is not used again after this on any path, and has the shape of the result
   = note: applied by the `lifetimes` analysis of the SIR interpreter
